> tl;dr
>
> - Benchmarked Azure DB for MySQL vs VM MySQL/MariaDB; MariaDB on a VM won consistently.
> - Ended up cutting DB‑bound latency by ~51% and reducing 503s by ~77% after migration.
> - Outcome enabled by close coordination between engineering, product and ops with strong technical leadership.

I shared the raw story and numbers in a longer post: [Drupal delayed by Azure MySQL](/posts/20250710-metrics-of-caution-azure-mysql/). This companion case study summarizes the decision, approach, and outcomes for readers who want the short version and the operational takeaways.

## Problem

After a cloud migration, non‑cached paths and admin UX regressed substantially under Azure DB for MySQL. Query times and p95 latency suggested the managed service was the bottleneck.

## Approach

- Load tests alternating endpoints (managed vs VM) to neutralize cache effects.
- Measured both user‑facing paths and CRUD micro‑benchmarks.
- Kept code/config static to isolate the database layer.

## Key decisions

- Move to MariaDB on a VM; keep backups and continuity plans tight.
- Reduce admin menu depth while diagnosing; later simplify the menu entirely.

## Outcomes

- ~51% faster on DB benchmarks; large wins on admin paths and heavy queries.
- ~77% fewer daily 503s; editor TTFB improved materially.

